            "logprobs": [],
            "text": "For an **upper-upscale hotel in the Caribbean or Latin America**, the owner should build the brand shortlist around the property’s format—urban/MICE, lifestyle conversion, traditional resort, or all-inclusive—rather than selecting solely by chain scale. Brand classifications also vary somewhat by country and data provider.\n\n## Recommended brand universe\n\n| Platform | Brands to prioritize | Best fit |\n|---|---|---|\n| **Marriott International** | **Marriott Hotels, Westin, Renaissance, Sheraton, Autograph Collection, Tribute Portfolio** | Large urban hotels, resorts, mixed-use projects and distinctive conversions. Autograph and Tribute are especially relevant when the owner wants to retain the property’s identity. Marriott positions these within its full-service “premium” portfolio. ([hotel-development.marriott.com](https://www.hotel-development.marriott.com/brands/premium-brands?utm_source=openai)) |\n| **Hilton** | **Hilton Hotels & Resorts, Curio Collection, Canopy by Hilton** | Hilton for conventional full-service hotels and resorts; Curio for independent/lifestyle conversions and destination resorts; Canopy for urban lifestyle projects. Hilton explicitly identifies Curio as upper-upscale, and both Curio and Canopy are expanding in CALA. ([stories.hilton.com](https://stories.hilton.com/uploads/2024/04/HLT.Q1-CORP-FactSheet-APR2024.pdf?utm_source=openai)) |\n| **Hyatt** | **Hyatt Regency, Hyatt Centric, The Unbound Collection, Destination by Hyatt, JDV by Hyatt** | Hyatt Regency for larger full-service/MICE assets; Centric and JDV for lifestyle hotels; Unbound and Destination for individualistic resorts and conversions. Hyatt classifies Regency, Centric and JDV as upper-upscale, while Unbound and Destination span upper-upscale and luxury. ([hyatt.com](https://www.hyatt.com/development/ourbrands?utm_source=openai)) |\n| **Accor** | **Pullman, MGallery Collection**; selectively **Swissôtel or Mövenpick** | Pullman for contemporary urban, convention and larger resort properties; MGallery for boutique, heritage and experiential assets. Accor explicitly positions MGallery in upper-upscale and has strong historical operating depth in Latin America. ([group.accor.com](https://group.accor.com/en/news-stories/collection-brands-handwritten-emblems-mgallery?utm_source=openai)) |\n| **IHG Hotels & Resorts** | **Hotel Indigo, Crowne Plaza**; selectively **Kimpton** | Hotel Indigo for locally inspired lifestyle hotels; Crowne Plaza for business/MICE demand; Kimpton when the project can support the upper boundary of upper-upscale or luxury lifestyle positioning. ([development.ihg.com](https://development.ihg.com/hotel-brands/kimpton-hotels-restaurants?utm_source=openai)) |\n| **Wyndham Hotels & Resorts** | **Wyndham Grand, Registry Collection Hotels** | Wyndham Grand for conventional upper-upscale resorts and urban hotels; Registry for independently styled resorts and conversions. Wyndham explicitly classifies both as upper-upscale, with Registry operating as a soft brand. ([development.wyndhamhotels.com](https://development.wyndhamhotels.com/upscale-lifestyle-hotel-brands/?utm_source=openai)) |\n| **Minor Hotels** | **NH Collection, nhow**; selectively **Tivoli** | Particularly relevant for urban Latin American markets and European/Latin American source demand. NH Collection is the principal premium option; nhow suits lifestyle projects; Tivoli may push into luxury. Minor has an established Latin American operating platform. ([minorhotels.com](https://www.minorhotels.com/en/development?utm_source=openai)) |\n\n## Best brands by project type\n\n### 1. Large urban, business or convention hotel\n\nFor a 200-plus-room hotel with meaningful meeting space, prioritize:\n\n- Hyatt Regency\n- Marriott Hotels\n- Hilton Hotels & Resorts\n- Westin\n- Sheraton\n- Renaissance\n- Pullman\n- Crowne Plaza\n- NH Collection\n\n**Best initial RFP group:** Hyatt Regency, Marriott, Hilton, Pullman and NH Collection. Add Westin or Renaissance where wellness or lifestyle differentiation is important.\n\n### 2. Boutique, heritage or conversion property\n\nFor an existing independent hotel, historic building or distinctive 80–250-key asset:\n\n- Autograph Collection\n- Tribute Portfolio\n- Curio Collection\n- The Unbound Collection by Hyatt\n- JDV by Hyatt\n- MGallery Collection\n- Hotel Indigo\n- Registry Collection Hotels\n- Hyatt Centric for a more standardized urban-lifestyle product\n\nThis category is often the most attractive for owners because a soft brand can preserve the hotel’s name, architecture and local identity while adding distribution and loyalty-system access. Accor reports that approximately half of its development projects are conversions, while Hilton, Marriott and Hyatt all actively position collection brands for independent properties. ([group.accor.com](https://group.accor.com/en/hotel-development/solutions-for-every-project?mdrv=group.accor.com&utm_source=openai))\n\n### 3. Caribbean or coastal resort\n\nFor a traditional European-plan resort:\n\n- Westin\n- Marriott Hotels\n- Autograph Collection\n- Hilton Hotels & Resorts\n- Curio Collection\n- Hyatt Regency\n- Destination by Hyatt\n- MGallery\n- Pullman\n- Wyndham Grand\n- Registry Collection\n\n**Best resort shortlist:** Curio, Autograph, Destination by Hyatt, Westin, MGallery and Wyndham Grand/Registry.\n\nThe collection brands are particularly useful when the destination, architecture and resort experience are more important than rigid brand uniformity.\n\n### 4. All-inclusive resort\n\nUpper-upscale all-inclusive projects require a somewhat different operator universe. Consider:\n\n- **Hyatt:** Hyatt Ziva, Hyatt Zilara and Dreams Resorts & Spas\n- **Hilton:** Curio Collection or Hilton Hotels & Resorts all-inclusive\n- **Marriott:** Autograph Collection, Tribute Portfolio or Marriott Hotels all-inclusive\n- **IHG:** Iberostar Beachfront Resorts\n- **Wyndham/Palladium:** Registry Collection, Grand Palladium and selected TRS structures\n- **Meliá:** Meliá Hotels & Resorts and selected Paradisus projects\n- **Barceló:** Barceló and selected Royal Level concepts\n- **Accor:** Pullman or Mövenpick where available\n\nSome of these are internally described as luxury rather than upper-upscale, but they may still be appropriate competitive-set candidates depending on room size, ADR, service intensity and food-and-beverage programming. Hyatt, for example, classifies Dreams and several of its other inclusive brands as “all-inclusive luxury.” ([hyatt.com](https://www.hyatt.com/development/ourbrands?utm_source=openai))\n\n## Practical priority shortlist\n\nIf the owner wants to approach only the strongest initial candidates, I would recommend:\n\n1. **Curio Collection by Hilton**\n2. **Autograph Collection**\n3. **Hyatt Regency**\n4. **The Unbound Collection by Hyatt**\n5. **Marriott Hotels or Westin**\n6. **Hilton Hotels & Resorts**\n7. **MGallery Collection**\n8. **Pullman**\n9. **Hotel Indigo**\n10. **NH Collection**\n11. **Wyndham Grand or Registry Collection**\n12. **Renaissance or Hyatt Centric**\n\nThe final five-to-eight-brand RFP should be adjusted as follows:\n\n- **Unique conversion:** Curio, Autograph, Unbound, MGallery, Hotel Indigo and Registry.\n- **Large urban/MICE hotel:** Hyatt Regency, Marriott, Hilton, Westin, Pullman and Crowne Plaza.\n- **Lifestyle city hotel:** Renaissance, Canopy, Hyatt Centric, JDV, Hotel Indigo and MGallery.\n- **Beach resort:** Curio, Autograph, Destination by Hyatt, Westin, MGallery and Wyndham Grand.\n- **All-inclusive:** Hyatt Inclusive Collection, Hilton/Curio, Marriott/Autograph, Iberostar, Registry/Palladium and Meliá.\n\n## What the owner should compare in the RFP\n\nBrand selection should ultimately be based on negotiated economics and expected contribution, including:\n\n- Brand-reservation and loyalty contribution by feeder market\n- Base, incentive, franchise, marketing and loyalty fees\n- Required property-improvement plan and opening capital\n- Brand-standard flexibility and waiver process\n- Franchise versus management availability\n- Owner termination and performance-test provisions\n- Area-of-protection or noncompete terms\n- Group, MICE and airline-crew sales capability\n- All-inclusive revenue-management and procurement expertise\n- Local technical-services and pre-opening resources\n- Compatibility with residences, villas or mixed-use components\n- Local-language support, payment systems, currency and tax structure\n\nFor most CALA projects, the strongest strategy is to compare **two conventional hard brands, three collection/lifestyle brands and one or two regional or all-inclusive specialists**, rather than assuming the largest global brand will necessarily produce the best owner return."
